A||||7||Gokart||Grease fittings||||8||
Z||000000||Gokart||01-09-2000||10:22 PM||||Did a lube on my GT today and noticed the lower grease fitting on the drivers side rear tire was missing. Got one off my parts car, but couldn't get it to screw in so I suspect it was broken off around the threads! Is there any way I can get grease in the rubber tube there or do I wait and get it fixed. Is it a big job? Noticed some of the rubber tubes were pretty bad as well. TIA for replys!||||reg||8
Z||000001||TOM||01-09-2000||11:03 PM||||Use a small easy out or a left hand drill to get the broken piece out.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WGraffiKs||01-11-2000||07:27 PM||||Tom,<BR>What's a "left hand drill"??||||reg||5
Z||000003||Gokart||01-11-2000||07:36 PM||||Was talking to a parts guy today at the Pontiac dealer I deliver to and he told me most auto parts stores sell a special tool to remove the broken fitting!? Anyone ever heard of this?||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||01-11-2000||08:57 PM||||EZ Outs or Easy Outs like Tom mentioned earlier. It kind of looks like a square tap and they come in several sizes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Harry||01-11-2000||10:33 PM||||Of course you could always cheat and just use the rubber snubber tip supplied with most good grease guns!||||reg||14
Z||000006||TOM||01-12-2000||12:33 AM||||True but with out the fitting is wont stay in like it is supposed to. It will leak back out the hole when it gets hot. A left hand drill is a drill bit ground to be used in the left or reverse direction. Sometimes when the bite the broken piece will screw out.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gokart||01-12-2000||06:15 AM||||Thanks guys!||||reg||14
